ate under the integral sign?\n\n1. $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n2. $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n3. $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$"] [17592186228874 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on\n\n$$\nR = [a,b]\\times [c,d]\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nexists and is continuous on $R$. Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d)$, and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x = \\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have\n$f_1(x,t) = t\\cos x$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_1$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t$.\n- For the second function, we have\n$f_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mid x - t \\mid}}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1)$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t$, since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R$, and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n- For the third function, we have\n$f_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}$.\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1]$, so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d]$.Note that $f_3$ is continuous on $R$. Computing the partial derivative, we have\n$\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}$\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$. Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0$. And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1]$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""]] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [[17592186228085 :answer-field/id] [17592186228085 :answer-field/key] [17592186228085 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
